<p>Credit card debt in the UAE can compound quickly if only minimum payments are made, since interest is typically calculated on the full statement balance rather than the remaining amount after a partial payment. Here's a practical approach to getting out of it faster.</p>

<h2>Step 1: List Every Card, Balance, and Rate</h2>
<p>Before choosing a strategy, get a complete picture: every card, its current balance, its interest rate, and its minimum payment. Most people underestimate their total balance until they actually add it up across cards — a bank statement export or a categorized view of your accounts makes this far faster than checking each app individually.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Choose a Payoff Method — and Stick to It</h2>
<p>Two well-tested approaches:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Avalanche method:</strong> Pay minimums on all cards, then throw every extra dirham at the highest-interest-rate card first. Mathematically optimal — saves the most in interest over time.</li>
  <li><strong>Snowball method:</strong> Pay minimums on all cards, then throw every extra dirham at the smallest balance first, regardless of interest rate. Less optimal mathematically, but the quick wins from closing small balances keep many people more motivated to continue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Neither is objectively "correct" — the best method is the one you'll actually stick with for months in a row.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Stop the Bleeding First</h2>
<p>Paying down debt while continuing to add new charges to the same cards is fighting against yourself. Where possible, use a debit card or cash for discretionary spending while focused on payoff, and reserve credit cards for cases where you can pay the statement in full.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Check for Balance Transfer or Consolidation Options</h2>
<p>Some UAE banks offer balance transfer promotions with reduced interest for an introductory period, and personal loans can sometimes carry lower effective rates than revolving credit card balances. These can be genuinely useful, but read the terms carefully — the rate that applies after any promotional period matters more than the headline offer.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Automate Extra Payments</h2>
<p>The households that pay off debt fastest usually automate an extra payment right after payday, before discretionary spending has a chance to eat into it — the same principle behind building an <a href="/blog/emergency-fund-calculator-uae">emergency fund</a> or hitting any other savings goal in the UAE.</p>

<h2>Track Progress, Don't Just Hope</h2>
<p>Debt payoff plans fail most often not because the math was wrong, but because progress becomes invisible and motivation fades. Seeing your real balances trend downward month over month — rather than estimating from memory — is one of the most effective ways to stay consistent. <a href="/">FinTrack's</a> Safe-to-Spend view accounts for committed debt payments before showing what's free to spend, so extra payments happen by default rather than by willpower alone.</p>

